# confusable-word.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M103): a word that is visible and lies — demonstrates
# `tflw check`'s teaching diagnostic (TF050: lexer, tflw M103 / review `M98d-02`).
#
# The other half of the Trojan Source class. `TF049` (hidden-character.tflw, next door) covers
# characters with *no glyph*. These have a glyph and it is somebody else's: a Cyrillic `а` (U+0430)
# is not a Latin `a`, and `"аdmin"` renders exactly like `"admin"` while comparing unequal to it.
#
# **The severity comes from the negative matchers.** In `is`/`equals` a confusable makes the test
# *fail*, which is loud and self-correcting. In `not equals`/`not contains` — the shape a
# leak-prevention assertion takes, and this suite has a real one at
# tests/.env-specific/webv2-admin.tflw:196 proving an internal comment is hidden from a non-owner —
# it makes the test **pass without asserting anything**, with no evidence in an editor, in a diff,
# in a pull request, or in `tflw check`. The first `expect` below is that exact shape.
#
# The rule's unit is one **word**, not one string (D178): a `.tflw` string is prose and prose is
# legitimately multilingual, so the bilingual line below stays legal — two scripts, no mixed word.
# Only scripts with Latin lookalikes count (D179): `"東京Tower"` mixes Latin and Han in one word and
# deceives nobody, because Han has no Latin homoglyphs. Common and Inherited never count, which is
# what keeps `—`, `§`, `…`, `→` and `×` off the rule.
#
# **Strings only** (D180), unlike `TF049`, which also fires in comments. A comment has no `\u{…}` to
# escape into, and a rule with no way to comply is a capability removed rather than a lint — so the
# Cyrillic in *this* comment is deliberate and legal: оk.
#
# **Not covered:** a word written entirely in one non-Latin script that still reads as Latin
# (`"аԁmіn"` in all Cyrillic). That needs the UTS #39 confusables table, and by shape it is
# indistinguishable from legitimate Russian data. Stated so the guarantee is not read as wider.
#
# The offending characters are invisible *as differences*. Read this file with
# `rg --pcre2 "[\u0400-\u04FF\u0370-\u03FF]" tests/.checkonly/confusable-word.tflw`, not by eye.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/confusable-word.tflw

test "a leak guard that passes without asserting anything"
  api GET /health
  # U+043E where a Latin `o` belongs: this assertion can never fail.
  expect body.status not equals "оk"
  # U+03BF (Greek omicron) inside an otherwise-Latin word.
  expect body.page not contains "lοgin"

test "the legal neighbours the rule must not touch"
  api GET /health
  # Two scripts, one string, no mixed word — a bilingual label, not a spoof.
  log "Willkommen — добро пожаловать"
  # Latin + Han in one word: Han has no Latin lookalikes.
  log "東京Tower"
  # Latin with diacritics, and Common-script punctuation.
  log "Zürich naïve café · Order — 42 × items → §3"
  # The escape hatch: the same Cyrillic letter, written so it says so in plain sight.
  log "\u{0430}dmin"
